// benchframe.hml — the per-stage frame bench and the budget regression gate. W4-13.
//
// CLAUDE.md §2 has documented this command since Wave 0:
//
//     hemlockc -O1 tools/benchframe.hml -o /tmp/bf && SDL_VIDEODRIVER=dummy /tmp/bf
//
// A Gate 3a verifier found the file did not exist, so the documented command
// was a lie. This is that file. It drives the REAL frame graph
// (`src/render/world_render.hml`) and the REAL simulation (`src/sim/sim.hml`)
// over a moving camera at the shipping triangle budget, prints the
// ARCHITECTURE.md §4 stage table with per-stage deltas, and EXITS 1 when a
// stage, a counter or the allocation gate regresses past its guard.
//
//   modes
//     (none)      bench + gate. exits 1 on any failure.   <- the CI form
//     --quick     fewer frames; a smoke test, still gated
//     --ablate    stage ablation: what each optional stage actually costs
//     --sweep     THE TRIANGLE-CEILING SWEEP. scales the drawn triangle count
//                 through the real frame path and reports the marginal cost per
//                 triangle and the count at which the frame crosses 16.67 ms,
//                 with read_pixels_rect excluded. See section 7b.
//     --csv       machine-readable stage table on top of the human one
//     --frames N  frames per trial (default 90)
//     --trials N  trials (default 7, RULE T)
//     --no-gate   print the table, never exit 1 (for exploring, never for CI)
//     --help
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW IT TIMES, AND WHY IT IS NOT NAIVELY __clock()
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// RULE T is "__clock() CPU time + minimum of 7". Both halves are used, but not
// in the same column, and the reason is measured rather than assumed:
//
//   * THE MINIMUM OF 7 IS USED EVERYWHERE. Preemption can only make a sample
//     slower, so the minimum stays near true cost while the mean drifts with
//     load (BUDGET_ACTUAL: +62 % naive, +29 % with the full recipe). Every
//     number in the stage table is a min over `--trials` independent trials of
//     `--frames` frames each, divided by the frame count.
//
//   * THE PER-STAGE COLUMN IS THE SDL PERFORMANCE COUNTER, not __clock(), and
//     that is deliberate. `tools/walk.hml` measured the two side by side on the
//     real display and recorded the result in its own source: __clock() is
//     PROCESS CPU time summed over every thread, so with a GL driver thread
//     alive it reported 12.2 ms of "render CPU" against a 12.1 ms wall frame
//     and attributed the driver's concurrent work to whichever stage happened
//     to be open. stats.hml — the shipping instrumentation, the thing the game
//     actually carries — is on the performance counter. Timing the stages on a
//     different clock from the one the game reports would bench something the
//     game does not run.
//
//   * __clock() IS STILL MEASURED, around the whole frame and around the sim
//     step, and printed as its own column with the CPU/wall skew. Under the
//     dummy video driver there is no driver thread, so the two agree; on the
//     real display they do not, and the printed skew is the evidence for the
//     paragraph above rather than a claim about it.
//
// Guards are set at ~2x the number measured under this box's permanent ~17/24
// core load, and every assertion prints BOTH numbers, as RULE T requires. A
// guard that false-fails trains everyone to ignore red, which costs more than
// the regression it was protecting against.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IT DRIVES
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// R7 forbids a tool from carrying its own frame graph, and it is right to: a
// bench against a private copy of the renderer measures the copy. This file
// defines no `frame_render`. It calls `frame_begin` / `frame_render` /
// `frame_present` — the same three the walkaround and the screenshot tool call
// — with the same caller-owned stages 6, 7 and 24 the import wall leaves with
// every caller (ARCHITECTURE §5.6b).
//
// The WORLD is benchframe's own: a deterministic ring of terrain chunks, props
// from the real `meshgen`, real FX cards and a real HUD, sized to land on the
// 2500-triangle budget. It is not walk.hml's art and does not try to be — what
// has to be identical is the SHAPE of the work (triangle count per layer, prop
// count, draw calls, texture runs), because that is what the stage costs are a
// function of. The art is what the frame LOOKS like; this file measures what it
// COSTS. `tools/shot.hml` owns the former.
//
// The SIM is the real one: `sim_new_cap` over the real worldgen chunk source,
// two players, eight steered agents, a lantern and a pickup, stepped at the
// fixed timestep through `sim_accum_advance` exactly as `main.hml` will.

